      <section id="introduction" class="section">
        <p><strong>Dimethyl ether aerosol propellant</strong> is a versatile, water-miscible liquefied gas propellant utilized across personal care, household, and industrial spray formulations. It works by expanding from a liquid state under pressure to maintain constant dispensing force while dissolving polar concentrates. This matters for aerosol manufacturers because it offers high solvency and low Global Warming Potential, enabling VOC reduction and reducing dependency on hydrocarbons. To understand how propellants behave in complete packaging systems, refer to our comprehensive guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/how-aerosol-cans-work" target="_top">how aerosol cans work</a>.</p>
        <p>As regulatory frameworks globally place tighter limits on Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs) and greenhouse gases, the aerosol industry has faced pressure to phase out traditional fluorocarbons and optimize volatile organic systems. Traditional liquefied hydrocarbon propellants, such as butane, isobutane, and propane mixtures, are highly effective hydrophobic solvents but fail to mix with polar concentrates. Dimethyl ether (DME), represented by the chemical formula CH₃OCH₃, bridges this gap. By offering a unique combination of dual-phase miscibility, strong organic solvency, and an excellent environmental profile, DME has established itself as an indispensable chemical tool for modern aerosol product development.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section id="properties" class="section">
        <h2>Physical and Chemical Properties of Dimethyl Ether</h2>
        <p>Dimethyl ether is the simplest ether compound, consisting of two methyl groups bonded to a central oxygen atom. At standard temperature and pressure, DME is a colorless, sweet-smelling, non-toxic gas. However, when pressurized, it easily liquefies, exhibiting a vapor pressure of approximately 5.1 bar (510 kPa) at 20°C. This pressure profile is ideal for aerosol packaging, as it sits comfortably within the safety tolerances of standard aluminum and tinplate canisters without requiring excessive wall thickness. For exact thermodynamic calculations and vapor pressure curves, consult the <a href="https://webbook.nist.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST WebBook</a>.</p>
        <p>Unlike standard hydrocarbons, the ether linkage in DME introduces a moderate dipole moment (1.30 D). This molecular polarity gives DME its distinctive ability to dissolve in both polar and non-polar liquid phases. The physical properties of DME must be carefully accounted for during the aerosol filling process, particularly its liquid density and boiling point. Key thermodynamic specifications are detailed below:</p>

