      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section id="solvency" class="section">
        <h2>Solvency and Aerosol Formulation Dynamics</h2>
        <p>In product development, the choice of propellant directly influences the solubility of active concentrates. When comparing DME vs LPG propellant, the most significant difference lies in their solvency power. DME has a Hildebrand solubility parameter of 18.6 MPa^1/2, placing it close to common solvents like ethyl acetate and acetone. Standard hydrocarbon propellants (LPG) are highly non-polar, with parameters around 14.0 MPa^1/2. Because of this high solvency, DME behaves not only as a pressurizing agent but also as a functional solvent. For a deeper understanding of solubility parameters and concentrate chemistry, read our guide on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-chemistry-101" target="_top">aerosol chemistry 101</a>.</p>
        <p>This solvency power is particularly valuable for hair sprays, personal deodorants, and industrial coatings. In these formulations, DME can dissolve film-forming resins, active antiperspirants, and industrial binders, eliminating the need for expensive co-solvents like absolute ethanol or methylene chloride. Furthermore, DME is unique among liquefied gas propellants due to its high water miscibility. DME can form a stable, single-phase solution with water at concentrations up to 34% by weight. This enables the design of water-based aerosol formulations that maintain fine atomization, lower raw material costs, and comply with strict environmental regulations.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Key Insight</strong>
          Dimethyl ether's water miscibility allows formulation chemists to introduce water directly into the liquid phase. The water acts as a carrier and reduces the overall VOC content, while the dissolved DME acts as a vapor-tap flash agent to ensure a dry, fine spray pattern.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="compatibility" class="section">
        <h2>Gasket and Valve Compatibility in DME Aerosols</h2>
        <p>A major engineering challenge in using DME is ensuring the chemical compatibility of the packaging components. Because of its polar ether structure, DME exhibits strong swelling effects on many common elastomers used in aerosol valve gaskets. For instance, standard ethylene propylene diene monomer (EPDM) and nitrile (NBR) rubbers absorb DME rapidly, causing them to swell, lose structural strength, and deform. This swelling can block the valve orifice, resulting in container leakage or valve failure. For a complete analysis of valve components and elastomer chemistry, see our detailed guide on the <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-valve-gasket-explained" target="_top">aerosol valve gasket materials</a>.</p>
        <p>To establish a reliable seal, valve manufacturers utilize alternative polymers. Neoprene (chloroprene) rubber, butyl rubber (IIR), or specialized fluorocarbon elastomers (Viton/FKM) are typically specified for DME systems. These elastomers restrict swelling to acceptable levels (typically under 15% volume change), maintaining sealing pressure around the valve cup and stem. Product developers must run long-term compatibility testing at elevated temperatures to verify gasket performance over the product's shelf life.</p>

        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--amber); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Never specify standard EPDM gaskets for formulations containing more than 5% dimethyl ether. Always use specialized chloroprene (Neoprene) or butyl rubber gaskets to prevent swelling, leakage, and spray failure.
        </div>
      </section>

