      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="toxicology" class="section">
        <h2>Toxicology and Human Exposure Profile</h2>
        <p>From an occupational health perspective, the dimethyl ether toxicology profile is highly favorable. DME has low acute toxicity when inhaled, with an LC50 in rodents exceeding 164,000 ppm over 4 hours. Extensive toxicity databases, including the National Institutes of Health <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem</a> database, classify DME as a non-mutagenic, non-carcinogenic substance that does not accumulate in human tissue. However, like all compressed gases, high concentrations can displace oxygen, presenting an asphyxiation hazard in poorly ventilated areas.</p>
        <p>For workplace safety, regulatory agencies have established occupational exposure limits. In both the United States and the European Union, the standard threshold limit value (TLV) is set at a time-weighted average (TWA) of 1000 ppm over an 8-hour shift. Inhalation of high levels of DME (above 50,000 ppm) can cause mild anesthesia, dizziness, or central nervous system depression. Direct contact with escaping liquefied gas also poses physical risks, such as severe cryogenic burns or frostbite to skin and eyes. Therefore, manufacturing facilities must feature robust ventilation and personal protective equipment protocols.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="flammability" class="section">
        <h2>Flammability Hazards, Storage, and Handling Compliance</h2>
        <p>Although DME has favorable toxicology, it is classified as a GHS Category 1 Flammable Gas. It forms explosive mixtures in air within flammability limits of 3.4% to 27.0% by volume. In manufacturing environments, these limits require strict engineering controls. Filling lines must utilize Class I, Division 1, Group D explosion-proof electrical fittings, pneumatic interlocks, and continuous hydrocarbon gas detection sensors. For a comprehensive overview of safety equipment, see our guide on <a href="/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide" target="_top">aerosol manufacturing plant design</a>.</p>
        <p>For warehouse safety, products formulated with DME must comply with local fire protection standards. Under the <a href="https://www.nfpa.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NFPA</a> 30B code (Code for the Manufacture and Storage of Aerosol Products), aerosol cans filled with DME are typically classified as Level 3 aerosols. This classification requires specialized storage configurations, including fire barriers, early-suppression fast-response (ESFR) water sprinklers, and designated containment areas to mitigate the risk of high-intensity fires.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="environmental" class="section">
        <h2>Environmental Profile: Low GWP and Sustainability Impact</h2>
        <p>The primary driver for the adoption of DME in modern aerosol formulations is its low environmental impact. DME has an ozone depletion potential of zero (ODP = 0) and a global warming potential of less than one (GWP &lt; 1). This is a significant improvement over traditional hydrofluorocarbons like HFC-134a, which has a GWP of approximately 1430. Sustainable guidelines from the British Aerosol Manufacturers' Association (<a href="https://www.bama.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BAMA</a>) encourage the use of DME to help manufacturers meet corporate greenhouse gas reduction goals.</p>
        <p>Under current regulatory frameworks, DME is classified as a Volatile Organic Compound (VOC). However, because of its water miscibility, formulation chemists can replace organic solvents with water, lowering the overall VOC weight percentage of the finished product. This dual role as solvent and propellant helps manufacturers meet VOC limits set by the California Air Resources Board (CARB) and European Union directives, ensuring global compliance. For a detailed comparison of propellant environmental performance, read our analysis on <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/propellant-influence-on-aerosol-performance" target="_top">propellant influence on aerosol performance</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <section id="faq" class="box" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 30px; margin: 40px 0;">
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 24px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; color: var(--text-primary);">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Why is dimethyl ether preferred over liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) in some aerosol formulations?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Dimethyl ether (DME) is preferred over standard LPG due to its exceptional solvency properties and high water miscibility. With a Hildebrand solubility parameter of 18.6 MPa^1/2, DME can dissolve polar active components and polymers, often eliminating the need for organic co-solvents like ethanol or acetone. Its ability to mix with water up to 34% by weight also allows for formulation of stable, water-based systems with reduced Volatile Organic Compound (VOC) emissions.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Which gasket materials are compatible with DME aerosol formulations?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">DME has high polar solvency and causes extreme swelling in standard ethylene propylene diene monomer (EPDM) and nitrile (NBR) rubbers. For reliable seals, specialized butyl rubber (IIR), chloroprene (neoprene) compounds with high filler content, or high-density fluorocarbon elastomers (Viton/FKM) are required to control swelling, prevent gasket deformation, and maintain crimp seal integrity.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What is the flammability hazard profile of dimethyl ether?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">DME is a highly flammable gas under GHS Category 1. It forms explosive mixtures in air within flammability limits of 3.4% to 27.0% by volume. In storage, products containing high percentages of DME are classified as Level 3 aerosols under NFPA 30B standards, requiring designated containment zones, advanced sprinkler systems, and Class I Division 1 Group D explosion-proof electrical installations.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Is dimethyl ether toxic to human health during routine exposure?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">DME possesses a low acute toxicity profile. Long-term animal inhalation studies demonstrate no carcinogenic, mutagenic, or teratogenic activity, with a no-observed-adverse-effect level (NOAEL) of 20,000 ppm. However, acute exposure to massive concentrations can lead to central nervous system depression, asphyxiation, or cardiac sensitization, necessitating occupational exposure limits like the 1000 ppm 8-hour TWA standard.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does DME impact the environment compared to fluorocarbon propellants?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">DME is an environmentally friendly alternative to fluorocarbon propellants like HFC-134a. It has zero Ozone Depletion Potential (ODP = 0) and an extremely low Global Warming Potential (GWP &lt; 1), whereas HFC-134a has a GWP of approximately 1430. DME is not restricted under the Kigali Amendment or Montreal Protocol, making it a sustainable choice for greenhouse gas reduction.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Can DME be formulated directly with water without separating?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Yes, DME is unique among liquefied gas propellants for its high water solubility. It can form a single, homogeneous liquid phase with water at concentrations up to 34% DME and 66% water by weight at room temperature and pressure. Beyond this limit, a two-phase system develops, which can be stabilized into a fine spray emulsion using appropriate non-ionic surfactants.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

